## 4. The two Red findings

### R1: the panel test helper never destructures the sharp result (blocker)

`tests/sprout-cast-panel.test.ts:217` reads a panel with:

```ts
async function rawPanel(file: string): Promise<any> {
  return await sharp(file).ensureAlpha().raw().toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });
}
```

Sharp returns `{ data, info }`. The helper returns that object as it is, so `raw.width`,
`raw.height`, and `raw.channels` are `undefined` inside `colourBounds`. Every pixel index is
`NaN`, every colour count is 0, and the four pixel cases fail for any script. A scratch copy of the
Red file with one debug line prints the shape:

```
DBG cell 24 {"maxX":-1,"maxY":-1,"count":0} raw undefined undefined undefined 5097600
```

The repo convention is `const { data, info } = await sharp(...).toBuffer({ resolveWithObject:
true })`, as in `tests/mom-kim-sheet.test.ts:66` and `tests/render-options.test.ts:94`.

The four cases that fail on this helper:

- `three cells share the 700 px band, row 780 baseline and row 80 crown`
- `bun pixels stay above the body band inside the 56 px headroom`
- `sheet is 1544 x 900 with five figures at one scale and one baseline`
- `1600 px drawing crops to 440 px while its head band fits`

A scratch probe with the corrected reader passes all four intended cases against the panels of
commit `41458a54`:

```
